{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe sibling emitter writes candidate fixture specs asserting that, for a real plan, a\ncriterion should fire or stay silent. Those assertions come from history, and history is not\ncurrent code. When a candidate disagrees with today's criterion there are two explanations — the candidate\nis wrong, or the criterion has drifted since the review — and nothing in the mined data\nseparates them.\n\nAdmitting an unverified candidate is worse than none: a wrong must-fire case encodes a false\npositive as required behavior and will fail a later, correct rubric fix. A single confirming run cannot settle it: the store holds 551 review pairs over\nbyte-identical material of which 208 flipped verdict, which is why every shipped spec uses\n`epochs: 3` with `gate: at_least(2)`.\n\n## Approach\n\nRun every candidate against current code before admitting it; keep disagreements as evidence.\n\n**Admission run.** For each mined candidate case, execute the criterion's real Pass-1 finder\ndirectly through its solver — `eval_solver.run_case(prompt_id, case, runner, repo_root)`, the\nsame finder `calibrate_criterion` drives internally (one `passes.pass1_chunk` call per case\nper run, `eval_solver.py:139-142`). The runner does NOT call `calibrate_criterion`, which only scores an already-persisted dataset\nit loads via `load_eval_spec(prompt_id)`; this run's scope is criteria with NO existing spec,\nso it drives the solver over the in-memory candidate case instead. Each case runs three times; `_fired(verdict)` (a finding is\npresent) gives the observed fire/no-fire per run. A candidate whose observed behavior matches its prediction (fire for a must-fire case, silent\nfor a must-not-fire) in at least two of three runs REPRODUCES. Three runs / two-of-three matches the shipped `at_least(2)` convention.\n\n**Disposition.** A reproducing candidate is kept in its criterion's dataset; a\nnon-reproducing candidate is withheld from the suite and written to a drift report at\n`docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/fixtures/drift-report.md`, recording the criterion, the\nhistorical (predicted) behavior, the observed current behavior, and the originating review. A\ncriterion's spec is written to `.rebar/evals/<prompt-id>.eval.yaml` only when its reproducing\ncandidates still form a balanced (both-sides-non-empty) dataset; otherwise the whole criterion is withheld to the drift report. The written spec is a plain\nstrict-valid eval spec with NO posture field: posture (blocking/advisory) is a routing concept\n(`default_posture` in `criteria_routing.json`), not part of the eval-spec schema, so the selector's tier is recorded in the admission report, never on the spec.\n\n**Budget enforcement.** The run pre-flights through\n`ledger.reserve(estimate_usd, ledger_path=<epic ledger>, cap_usd=250.0)` using the\nparameterized ledger from the sibling ledger child, against this epic's own ledger file\n`docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/fixtures/ledger.jsonl`, and `finalize()`s priced usage per\ncriterion. The per-criterion estimate key is chosen from its `exec` tier in\n`criteria.effective_routing('.', gate_key='plan_review')`: an `AGENT` tier maps to\n`criteria-eval-agent`; `1-TURN`, `2-STEP` and `DET` map to `criteria-eval-cheap`. A `DET`\ncriterion's finder is deterministic and issues no priced model call, so it is finalized at its\nreal (zero-cost) usage. Model parity is pinned to the production Pass-1 class per ADR 0109.\n\n**Failure disposition.** A criterion is atomic: its spec is written only after all of its\ncandidates finish their three runs, so a partial criterion never lands. A transient model or\ntransport error is RETRYABLE and retried within the run. `BudgetExceeded` from the pre-flight\nis FATAL and non-retryable — a retry would breach the same ceiling. On a fatal error the run stops with completed criteria admitted and their spend reconciled\ninto the ledger (via `reconcile`, keyed by `run_id`), so a re-run resumes at the first\nuncompleted criterion; when a transient failure and the ceiling both apply, the ceiling wins.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe admission runner, its disposition logic, the drift-report writer, and the `.rebar/evals/`\nspec files it produces. Only criteria with no existing spec are written, so `load_eval_spec`\n(`eval.py:69-78`) never resolves a mined file in place of a packaged one.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Repairing any drift the report finds; each entry becomes a follow-on ticket linked\n  `discovered_from` the parent epic.\n- Editing any file under `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/`.\n- Re-running admission on a schedule (the sibling self-healing loop owns that).\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tests drive the disposition logic through a stubbed solver returning scripted fire and\nno-fire sequences, so the admit/withhold boundary is deterministic without a model. Separate\nunit tests cover the budget refusal against the real `ledger.reserve`, partial-criterion\natomicity, resume-without-recharge via `reconcile`, strict validity of every written spec, and\nthe no-shadowing invariant. One live run against the pinned production model exercises the real path end to end (three\nruns per case, two-of-three threshold declared before the run).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Against a must-fire candidate, a scripted run sequence of (fire, fire, silent) admits the\n      candidate into its criterion's written dataset; verified by `pytest` asserting the spec's\n      dataset contains the candidate's case.\n- [ ] Against a must-fire candidate, a scripted run sequence of (fire, silent, silent) withholds\n      the candidate and writes a drift-report entry naming the criterion, its predicted\n      behavior, its observed behavior, and the originating review; verified by `pytest`\n      asserting the report entry fields.\n- [ ] When the ledger file already holds spend within the ceiling's reserve, the run raises\n      `BudgetExceeded` and issues zero model calls; verified by `pytest` asserting the exception\n      and a call-counting stub reporting `0`.\n- [ ] Every written spec returns zero errors from `validate_eval_spec(spec, strict=True)`;\n      verified by `pytest` invoking the real validator over each written file.\n- [ ] The set of written filenames shares no member with `ls src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/`;\n      verified by `pytest` asserting the intersection is empty.\n- [ ] A criterion whose candidate runs raise before all of them finish writes no\n      `.eval.yaml` for that criterion; verified by `pytest` asserting no partial spec file\n      exists after a mid-criterion failure.\n- [ ] A re-run after a fatal stop re-charges no already-finalized criterion; verified by\n      `pytest` asserting `reconcile` returns the existing ledger row for a completed `run_id`\n      and the call-counting stub reports zero new calls for it.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A live admission run has completed over the full candidate set with the\n      Pass-1 model pinned to its production class, and this epic's ledger records total spend\n      greater than zero and at most 250.0 with a per-pass model id on every row.\n      provenance: environment=developer workstation against the production Bedrock chain;\n      principal=the configured Bedrock identity used by the plan-review gate;\n      privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — the run must elicit\n      real Pass-1 behavior, which no offline analysis can substitute for.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Each drift-report entry has a corresponding ticket linked\n      `discovered_from` the parent epic.\n      provenance: environment=the rebar tickets store; principal=the session's configured\n      rebar assignee; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — ticket\n      creation is a store write, not a repository file.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n`make lint`, `make typecheck` and `make test` pass; the live admission run has completed; the\ndrift report and the admitted specs are committed; ledger spend is at most $250.\n\n## Budget\n\n**Hard cap $250** (see above). Central estimate $112 across uncovered criteria."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788418920507252001,"uuid":"cdba973c-b107-49f0-87c4-b201219be5e4"}